WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.569
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:11.629
In 1987, President Ronald Reagan
famously demanded Gorbachev,

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:16.681
the President of the Soviet Union,
to tear down that wall.

00:00:16.681 --> 00:00:22.660
His speech laid the foundation for
this moment for two years later.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.891
Thousands of East Germans
camped outside the German

00:00:26.891 --> 00:00:31.794
embassy in Hungary demanding
access across the border, and

00:00:31.794 --> 00:00:37.290
three fundamental things,
freedom, safety and reunification.

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.046
Now, I wasn't born yet.

00:00:40.046 --> 00:00:46.329
Because of that moment, when I grew up
in Berlin, there were no more walls,

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:53.217
and another even questioned the right to
safety and freedom, until this happens.

00:00:53.217 --> 00:00:59.726
Four years ago, thousands of people were
stranded at the Hungarian border yet

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:05.050
again, but this time it wasn't
German citizens who pleaded for

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.501
the world to pay attention,
it was Syrian refugees.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:13.262
This sparked a movement across my country.

00:01:13.262 --> 00:01:18.744
Images like this,
of people welcoming refugees into Germany,

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:20.782
went around the world.

00:01:20.782 --> 00:01:26.620
I was so proud, so I decided to
leave my corporate job in the UK and

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.814
move back to Berlin to support
my government in absorbing

00:01:31.814 --> 00:01:36.271
the 1 million refugees
that ended up arriving.

00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:45.021
That moment changed my life, since then
I've met some incredible refugees.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:51.016
And Wright was some of the most dedicated
people I know who try to support them.

00:01:51.016 --> 00:01:58.897
Their strengths and resilience has become
my source of inspiration and drive.

00:01:58.897 --> 00:02:04.187
But at the same time,
the public conversation has changed.

00:02:04.187 --> 00:02:09.973
The outpouring of support has
turned into protest and fear.

00:02:09.973 --> 00:02:15.092
Instead of tearing down walls,
we are building them.

00:02:15.092 --> 00:02:22.301
This scares me, I don't fear that we
have lost control of our boarders,

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:28.226
I fear that we have lost control
of our fundamental values.

00:02:28.226 --> 00:02:30.401
What has happened?

00:02:30.401 --> 00:02:32.657
Let’s atart with the basics.

00:02:32.657 --> 00:02:36.725
There are 68 million
refugees in the world today.

00:02:36.725 --> 00:02:41.328
These are people that have been
forced to flee from their homes

00:02:41.328 --> 00:02:43.991
because of conflict and violence.

00:02:43.991 --> 00:02:44.884
Take Syria,

00:02:44.884 --> 00:02:49.841
a country that has become synonymous
with the world's refugee crisis.

00:02:49.841 --> 00:02:55.058
The Syrian war started nine
years ago with the Arab Spring.

00:02:55.058 --> 00:02:59.698
After a 13-year-old boy was tortured and
murdered for

00:02:59.698 --> 00:03:02.823
writing a pro-Arab Spring graffiti,

00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:07.380
people went out on the streets to protest,
peacefully.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.802
But President Bashar Al Assad
responded with gunfire.

00:03:11.802 --> 00:03:16.572
It sparked one of the deadliest
civil wars in our modern times,

00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:22.692
leaving 500,000 people dead, and
12 million forcefully displaced.

00:03:22.692 --> 00:03:27.575
That is two-thirds of the entire country.

00:03:27.575 --> 00:03:33.100
These are people who stood
up to demand freedom,

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.078
safety, and democracy.

00:03:36.078 --> 00:03:38.915
They fight for the values we preach.

00:03:38.915 --> 00:03:47.661
And we ignored them until they
literally showed up at our borders.

00:03:47.661 --> 00:03:50.019
At the height of the crisis,

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:55.924
sometimes thousands of refugees came
into just Berlin in a given week.

00:03:55.924 --> 00:03:59.847
On one of my first days working for
the government there,

00:03:59.847 --> 00:04:04.686
I went to visit an old decommissioned
airport at the heart of the city.

00:04:04.686 --> 00:04:09.368
The local state office had resorted
to seizing whatever they could

00:04:09.368 --> 00:04:13.642
find as shelters,
because there simply wasn't any space.

00:04:13.642 --> 00:04:18.590
That included school gyms and
the old airport's foyer.

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:21.042
But what I found was this.

00:04:21.042 --> 00:04:27.193
1,000 bunk beds cramped into what
look like an industrial hall.

00:04:27.193 --> 00:04:32.317
2,000 people lived here,
people from different cultures,

00:04:32.317 --> 00:04:36.077
different families,
from all over the worlds.

00:04:36.077 --> 00:04:41.633
The echo made the noise
level almost unbearable.

00:04:41.633 --> 00:04:46.067
There is no peace and quiet, no privacy.

00:04:46.067 --> 00:04:50.057
Some of the people who came looking for
freedom and

00:04:50.057 --> 00:04:53.670
safety had to stay here for
almost two years.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.353
They were safe, yes, but free?

00:04:57.353 --> 00:04:59.488
Not really.

00:04:59.488 --> 00:05:04.770
I remember a social worker who came and
pleaded with us to help

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:10.064
a refugee who was severely disabled and
suffered from PTSD.

00:05:10.064 --> 00:05:16.303
Placing him here was cruel, not just for
him, but really for everyone.

00:05:16.303 --> 00:05:22.749
Imagine trying to sleep whilst he
was enjoying his night terrors.

00:05:22.749 --> 00:05:29.448
He needed individual care, but
we simply weren't able to provide it.

00:05:29.448 --> 00:05:35.056
If there's one thing I learned that day,
it is that no one chooses this life,

00:05:35.056 --> 00:05:38.523
you're placed in it if
you're out of options.

00:05:41.518 --> 00:05:44.488
And there's something else I learned.

00:05:44.488 --> 00:05:50.476
If you're facing this humanitarian crisis,
it forces you to make tradeoffs.

00:05:50.476 --> 00:05:54.851
Who gets prioritized for
individual care, and who doesn't?

00:05:54.851 --> 00:05:58.658
Who gets to stay, and who doesn't?

00:05:58.658 --> 00:06:03.574
All of these decisions have
a tremendous human cost,

00:06:03.574 --> 00:06:08.500
and not just to the refugees,
but really to all of us.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.951
Accommodating refugees
requires a sacrifice.

00:06:12.951 --> 00:06:17.625
And some of it might be small,
like getting used to sharing your public

00:06:17.625 --> 00:06:21.203
spaces with people who look
really different to you.

00:06:21.203 --> 00:06:24.965
Or giving up your school gym for
two years.

00:06:24.965 --> 00:06:29.292
And some as big,
like having government resources

00:06:29.292 --> 00:06:32.822
diverted away from your local projects.

00:06:32.822 --> 00:06:38.236
It really is no wonder that
the cycle of history always follows

00:06:38.236 --> 00:06:42.930
the same pattern when it
comes to refugee movements.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:48.300
In 1938, 67% of Americans were opposed

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:55.558
to granting refuge to Jewish
people fleeing Nazi persecution.

00:06:55.558 --> 00:07:00.454
My own grandma came to
Germany as a refugee.

00:07:00.454 --> 00:07:04.425
She was German, but
she grew up in the Sudetenland,

00:07:04.425 --> 00:07:08.405
a small strip between Czechoslovakia and
Germany.

00:07:08.405 --> 00:07:10.093
After World War II,

00:07:10.093 --> 00:07:15.665
all the Germans living there had
a day to pack their bags and leave.

00:07:15.665 --> 00:07:21.403
Even though she shared a nationality and
a language with the people

00:07:21.403 --> 00:07:26.330
in her new hometown,
she was not greeted with kindness.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:34.382
My point is, this is a vicious cycle of
history every generation perpetuates.

00:07:34.382 --> 00:07:35.644
And this crisis really is no different.

00:07:35.644 --> 00:07:42.104
If we judge previous generations on
whether or not they took a stance,

00:07:42.104 --> 00:07:46.383
will future generations
not do the same to us?

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:55.537
I worry because I know that
the sacrifice is painful.

00:07:55.537 --> 00:08:00.297
While I was working in government
I organized a town hall meeting in

00:08:00.297 --> 00:08:03.527
one of the most liberal
districts of Berlin,

00:08:03.527 --> 00:08:08.385
where you can find welcome refugee
posters on every street corner.

00:08:08.385 --> 00:08:13.247
We had to ask the residents to
give up a tiny piece of their

00:08:13.247 --> 00:08:17.809
large neighborhood park for
a new refugee shelter.

00:08:17.809 --> 00:08:21.640
It turns out the people were furious.

00:08:25.335 --> 00:08:29.421
I know that we can't take everyone in.

00:08:29.421 --> 00:08:33.313
But that is not what
we're debating right now,

00:08:33.313 --> 00:08:36.824
instead we're turning a blind eye again.

00:08:36.824 --> 00:08:40.189
We're coming out with all
the creative arguments for

00:08:40.189 --> 00:08:42.815
why we don't have the resources to help.

00:08:42.815 --> 00:08:45.619
We are we not the best place to help?

00:08:45.619 --> 00:08:49.564
Why, in fact,
isolation is the best answer?

00:08:49.564 --> 00:08:54.471
We are back at the vicious
cycle of history.

00:08:54.471 --> 00:08:58.028
I know that we can break it.

00:08:58.028 --> 00:09:03.083
But for that,
I'm asking you to make a sacrifice.

00:09:03.083 --> 00:09:09.330
To stand up for
the values I know we all share.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:11.507
And that might not be today.

00:09:11.507 --> 00:09:17.811
I'm asking you to remember this talk when
the time comes for you to make a choice.

00:09:17.811 --> 00:09:24.873
Do I truly open my arms,
even if it costs me, or do I look away?

00:09:24.873 --> 00:09:29.988
And that cost might be small,
like speaking out publicly,

00:09:29.988 --> 00:09:36.347
even if it's risky, or giving up a tiny
space in your neighborhood park.

00:09:36.347 --> 00:09:41.122
Or it might be big,
like going out of your way to create

00:09:41.122 --> 00:09:46.125
opportunities for
refugees in your future companies.

00:09:46.125 --> 00:09:51.583
Every decision, every action counts.

00:09:51.583 --> 00:09:56.978
Previous generations might have
not gotten it all right, but

00:09:56.978 --> 00:10:03.317
our countries share history in
literally tearing down walls together.

00:10:03.317 --> 00:10:08.112
I don't want to be the generation
that builds them back up.

00:10:08.112 --> 00:10:09.163
Thank you.

00:10:09.163 --> 00:10:15.443
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:15.443 --> 00:10:17.371
[MUSIC]

